"""Original export acceptance exercise with dated official sources."""

def publish_export_guide(page, cfg):
    page('/ai-tools/check-free-ai-export/', 'Can you export and edit a free AI result? | '+cfg['brand'], 'Use practice notes to check that an exported AI answer stays editable and keeps the right values.', '<section class="page-hero task-hero"><p class="eyebrow">FREE AI TOOLS / FINISH THE TASK</p><h1>Can you export and edit the result?</h1><p class="lede">Check the saved result before committing to a free AI tool: export a small sample, reopen it in your intended editor, change one value, and compare it with the input. Make sure the file works, not just the answer in the chat.</p></section><article class="prose">\n<h2>Start with the destination</h2><p>For a message draft, editable text may be enough. For a table, you need the values to stay attached to their row and column labels. For a handout, inspect the final page layout as well. Write down the destination and the action you must perform there before generating a long answer. Check the export option in your own account; export access can differ by plan.</p>\n<figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/ai-export-check.svg" width="760" height="600" alt="Four checkpoints: define the destination, export a small sample, reopen and edit, compare with the original input."/><figcaption>Save a sample, reopen it, edit it and check it.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>A documented route: Gemini to Docs or Sheets</h2><p>Google documents a signed-in export route. Below a Gemini response, select Share &amp; export, then Export to Docs to create a new document in Drive. For a response containing a table, Export to Sheets appears at the table\'s bottom right; tables containing images cannot use this route. Options vary by app and, for Workspace accounts, by settings and availability. The destination service\'s terms apply. See <a href="https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/14184041?hl=en" rel="noopener">Google\'s export instructions</a>. These steps come from Google’s documentation; we have not tested this export in an account.</p>\n<h2>Try these Maple pop-up practice notes</h2><div data-original="fixture"><p>These invented notes include details that are easy to lose: a date, a minus sign, two similar names and a missing owner. Use them for practice instead of private client material.</p><blockquote>Project: Maple pop-up<br>Date: 2026-10-18<br>People: Mina prepares signs; Nina checks the stock list.<br>Stock: blue mugs 12; green mugs 7.<br>Adjustment: -3.50 USD (refund, not a new charge).<br>Delivery owner: UNKNOWN.<br>Do not invent an owner or change the date.</blockquote><p>Ask for a short project update and a two-column stock table. Keep the person assignments in the update. Require the adjustment to retain its minus sign, currency and refund meaning. Tell the tool to preserve UNKNOWN literally. Export the answer using the route you intend to use for your real task.</p><p>Save a copy of both these input notes and the answer before making corrections. Give the exported file a distinctive name such as maple-export-check-2026-10-05. Reopen the destination independently, rather than relying on a preview inside the chat. If your workflow requires a local file, download that format and reopen that file too.</p></div>\n<h2>Check the saved file</h2><div data-original="acceptance"><p>Use one row per check. If an option is unavailable, mark that step blocked. Record the app, visible plan label, date and export destination with the checklist so a later change does not silently overwrite today\'s result.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Expected result</th><th>Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>People</td><td>Mina signs; Nina stock. No swapped assignments.</td><td>Pass / repair / blocked</td></tr><tr><td>Date</td><td>2026-10-18 stays exact.</td><td>Pass / repair / blocked</td></tr><tr><td>Money</td><td>-3.50 USD remains a refund.</td><td>Pass / repair / blocked</td></tr><tr><td>Table</td><td>Blue mugs 12; green mugs 7 in the correct rows.</td><td>Pass / repair / blocked</td></tr><tr><td>Missing fact</td><td>Delivery owner stays UNKNOWN.</td><td>Pass / repair / blocked</td></tr><tr><td>Editing</td><td>Change blue mugs from 12 to 13; save, close and reopen. The other row remains 7.</td><td>Pass / repair / blocked</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The editing check is an intentional change made after comparison. Keep the original 12-unit version and label the edited copy as a demonstration; do not mistake that deliberate edit for the input\'s true stock count. If you only need a read-only handout, replace the editing check with your actual requirement, such as legible printing and complete page boundaries.</p></div>\n<figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/ai-export-repair.svg" width="760" height="600" loading="lazy" alt="Repair record with input fact, saved result, correction, and a reopen check. Example: a lost minus sign changes a refund into a charge."/><figcaption>Example: a lost minus sign turns a refund into a charge.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>Repair one failure at a time</h2><div data-original="repair"><p>Suppose the exported table shows 3.50 USD while your notes say -3.50 USD. This is an invented error for the exercise. Record: input fact = refund -3.50 USD; saved result = positive 3.50 USD; consequence = the sign no longer describes the intended adjustment; repair = restore the sign and refund label in the destination.</p><p>After the repair, save and reopen again. Check the amount in the file you will actually send, not just the corrected chat message. Keep the correction in a short log alongside the file. If the table\'s layout is the only problem, repair the column widths in the destination and check that the values still match their headings; do not ask AI to recreate correct numbers merely to improve appearance.</p><p>If export is unavailable, record the exact blocked step. For a small text-only draft, copying the text into your own editor may be a suitable fallback. Note that you copied it manually, then run the same checks. A screenshot is not a substitute when your requirement is editable text. Decide whether the remaining manual work fits your task before choosing a different tool or paying for a plan.</p></div>\n<h2>Recheck the export when its source changes</h2><p>Compare the current input with the file you will send. For the Maple exercise above, make a separately labelled revision: change blue mugs from 12 to 15 and assign delivery to Oren. Keep the earlier input as version A. Reopen the old export and look for its 12-unit row and UNKNOWN owner. Correct both against version B, save a new copy and reopen it. Do not confuse this source revision with the separate editing demonstration that changed 12 to 13.</p><figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/source-version-route.svg" width="760" height="650" loading="lazy" alt="Check the original document, the source available to AI, and the final saved result separately."/><figcaption>After a source revision, reopen the destination too.</figcaption></figure><p>Google’s <a href="https://support.google.com/gemininotebook/answer/16262519?hl=en" rel="noopener">Gemini Notebook notes help</a> currently describes exporting a note to a new Google Doc. Edits there do not sync back to the original note, and sharing permissions do not carry over. Saved response notes can now be edited after citations are removed. On mobile, notes can be viewed and used in chat, but note editing is not supported. Reviewed 2026-10-08; these instructions concern that notes route, not every AI export.</p><p>For a worked version comparison, <a href="/ai-tools/check-ai-source-updates/">check source updates before reusing an answer</a>.</p>\n<h2>Questions before you use the file</h2><details><summary>Does a PDF export prove the result is editable?</summary><p>No. Match the export format to the editing action you need and try that action in the destination. A PDF that looks right may satisfy a handout task while failing your spreadsheet task.</p></details><details><summary>Why does a table look different after export?</summary><p>Appearance and data correctness are separate checks. Compare labels and values first, then adjust layout in your editor. A <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/GoogleGeminiAI/comments/1pn33wz/why_does_exporting_gemini_tables_to_google_docs/" rel="noopener">community discussion about Gemini table exports</a> reports formatting frustration; it is an individual experience, not proof of a universal defect.</p></details><details><summary>Do I need to test every AI tool?</summary><p>No. Apply it to the one tool, account and destination you intend to use. Leave any checks you did not run marked untested and note the repairs you made.</p></details><p><a href="/start/">Draft your first useful AI result</a> · <a href="/ai-tools/scanned-pdf-to-text/">Prepare readable PDF text</a> · <a href="/free-ai/">Compare free entry points</a></p><p>Reviewed 2026-10-05. The Maple notes, checklist and diagrams are our practice material. The product instructions come from documentation, not an account-level export test.</p></article>', lastmod='2026-10-08')
